{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 40, :comments ["<b>And these are the names of the chiefs of Esau</b> — At first (Genesis 36:15–19) He counted his grandsons who were chiefs in that generation, and afterward his seed prospered into kingship, and afterward their kingship ceased and they again appointed these chiefs as heads. Thus it is stated in Chronicles (1 Chronicles 1:51): 'And Hadad died, and the chiefs of Edom were: chief Timna' — thus Rashi explained (Rashi on Genesis 36:40). And what it says, 'by their families, by their places, by their names, by their settlements in the land of their possession' (Genesis 36:43), is because the first group consisted of all the brothers being chiefs, dwelling together in one city, ruling over the people as one; or like the manner of tribal princes and heads of fathers' houses. But these latter ones were chiefs of their families, for each one was chief over all the families of the sons of Esau and in all the places of their settlements, and he alone was called 'chief' in his generation — no other was so called in all the land of their possession, for they were like kings of their land but did not sit upon a throne, nor was royal honor given them. And this passage was written by way of prophecy according to many authorities (Rashi, Ibn Ezra, and Radak). But this is not correct, for why would prophecy mention these, and until what time would it count them and interrupt with them? Rather, the correct view is that all of these were before the giving of the Torah, and we say that they ruled as one, and the meaning of 'by their places' would be that each one ruled in his own place; or that their rule lasted only a brief time, and 'the years of the wicked are cut short' (Proverbs 10:27)."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 36, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:40:41.188043577Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}